Z||000009||GarethWright||10-09-2001||01:53 AM||||the 2nd pic is about how my GT looks and I personally haven't done anything to it, don't know what the previous owner did other than herb adams sway bar in the back.  My tires look like they have taller sidewalls than the ones you have, so that may be it.  Either way, it looks much better in the 2nd pic!||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnF||10-09-2001||08:10 AM||||Why does the rear tire look "Volkswagonish" in the rear?  Looks like it would result in very uneven wear with wheel being canted inward at the bottom.  Could just be an illusion, though.||||reg||5
Z||000011||Alex4mula||10-09-2001||09:19 AM||||Hey qwikgta, I haven't been in this side of the forum until today. Cool, two post of the same thing at different places! Your car looks nice but I don't know if it is the picture but I see the front didn't lower as much. I did 1.5 in front and probably 1.25 in the rear. But then Formula and coupe springs are different. Stii looks nece  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>I see you had the same problems I did. My passenger side upper strut bolt didn't fit also. I had to open the strut hole with a grinding bit. PIA! But I didn't need a spring compressor once the springs were cut. Mmmm... Maybe the diff in springs again. <BR>If someone is going to do this measure twice and cut once. I wouldn't do more than 1.75 coils in front and 1.5 in the back. Once the spring is out you will see why. More than that and the spring gets like 3" shorter. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000012||x-thumpr-x||10-09-2001||11:23 AM||||Don't kow if this can be down on a fiero, but a friend cut his springs on a rabbit with them still on the car. He just raised the car till the wheels are off the ground so the shocks are fully stretched out. Then he cut 1 coil off each spring. Gotta watch your hands though, got a good laugh when the grinder finally cut through the spring, the spring suddenly expanded to it's new size while the cut-off peice flung out of the way. Didn't fly off the car though since it's was 1 ring and still wrapprd around the strut. Just have to make another cut on it to remove the peice. "This was on a VW rabbit, might not work on the fiero!"||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||10-09-2001||11:26 AM||||Definitely won't work. Extremely dangerous too.<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red & White (:||||reg||1
Z||000014||FieroHarry||10-09-2001||08:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>Why does the rear tire look "Volkswagonish" in the rear?  Looks like it would result in very uneven wear with wheel being canted inward at the bottom.  Could just be an illusion, though.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>John he hasnt had the new aligment done yet.<BR>You have to do this after taking apart the rear suspension. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Looks good Rob!<BR>Mine was cut at one turn, wish I had done 1 3/4 myself...<BR><P>------------------<BR><BR><B>"If you want to blend in, take the bus!"</B>||||reg||1
Z||000015||qwikgta||10-09-2001||09:59 PM||||Here is some feedback.<BR> - top pic is before, if you look at the line that is made by the molding you will see that in the front if you drew a line from front to rear, the line would be above the tire, in the after shot it would hit the tire.  Also, I am running 205/50/16 F and 225/50/16 rear. Big diff from the 195/70/14 stock<BR> - the primary reason I did anything was to install the KYB shock/struts and rear swaybar.    When I had the rears off and the fronts off I was going to just put on the KYB's and then I read a thread about cutting coils, so I went and did it.  I asked how much but got no answer so I WAGed (Wild A$$ Guess)ed it.  <BR> - yes, there is not much diff in the rear but I did get an inch.  I was not sure how much to cut so I only cut about a coil and a quarter.  I was afraid that when I was done I would be a$$ low so I wanted to err on the side of high.  <BR> - Yes the driver side rear is in need of alignment, all four tires need it but the back drivers side is the worst.  I drove it around the block and it is tight.  <BR> - I am thinking about ripping it all off again and cutting it some more before I take it in for an alignment.  If I don't I will make coilovers and the front a winter project too. (along with the poly)<P> I know you may not see the drop but when I stand next to the car it is way lower in the front and so-so in the rear.  I really wanted to get rid of the nose high ride. <P> Thanks for the input<P>Rob<p>[This message has been edited by qwikgta (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
